       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "El museo no tiene columnas en el centro; es decir, la plaza de abajo queda libre.",
+          "english": "The museum has no columns in the middle; that is, the square below stays free."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Los barrios del ABC tienen muchas fábricas; o sea, allí trabajan muchos obreros.",
+          "english": "The ABC districts have many factories; I mean, many workers work there."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "São Paulo recibió inmigrantes de muchos países; en otras palabras, es una ciudad de origen mixto.",
+          "english": "São Paulo received immigrants from many countries; in other words, it is a city of mixed origins."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "'Por el contrario' exige que la proposición precedente contenga una negación explícita o implícita: 'No disminuyó... por el contrario, creció'."
+      "content": "The new wording comes after the marker, and a comma or semicolon comes before it. Do not use these markers to add a new fact: for that you know *además* from A2 Unit 28. If the second part is the opposite of the first, you need a contrast connector such as *sin embargo*, not a restatement."
     }
